Let each machine choose who writes the notes

Transcription has been local for a while, but the notes were hardwired to the
Claude Code CLI: a coworker without a Max subscription got a working recorder
and nothing else. Notes now go through a provider layer - the CLI, an Anthropic
key, an OpenRouter key, or any endpoint that speaks /chat/completions.

That last one is deliberate. If Yapper ever points at one official API for
everybody, it is an entry in llm.js and nothing else: the three places that
generate text (summarize, regenerate, auto-title) call one function and do not
know who answers.

The API key is sealed with the OS keystore (DPAPI on Windows, Keychain on
macOS) instead of sitting readable in settings.json, and it never leaves the
main process - the renderer only ever learns whether one is set. Where a
platform has no keystore, the UI says the key is stored unencrypted rather than
implying protection it does not have.

Errors are translated into something worth reading: a 401 says the key was
rejected, a 429 says rate limited or out of credit, a 404 says that model does
not exist. A Test connection button makes a 16-token round trip so Settings can
say "working" rather than "saved".

Tests: test-llm.js runs the providers against a fake server, checking routing,
payload shape and each error path. test-keystore.js proves the key does not
appear in what gets stored, under both a stand-in and the real system keystore.
test-llm-ui.js boots the actual app and drives the settings row end to end.
test-ipc-wiring.js checks all 49 bridge channels have a counterpart, which is
the failure that otherwise waits until someone clicks. npm test runs everything
that needs neither a model nor a GPU.

